1
00:01:01,609 --> 00:01:03,486
<i>Central Services</i>

2
00:01:03,694 --> 00:01:06,530
<i>We do the work, you do the pleasure</i>

3
00:01:06,656 --> 00:01:09,784
<i>Hi, there. I want to talk to you about ducts.</i>

4
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:13,329
<i>Do your ducts seem old-fashioned,</i>

5
00:01:13,746 --> 00:01:15,248
<i>out-of-date?</i>

6
00:01:15,498 --> 00:01:18,459
<i>Central Services' new duct designs</i>

7
00:01:18,542 --> 00:01:21,879
<i>are now available
in hundreds of different colors</i>

8
00:01:21,963 --> 00:01:24,340
<i>to suit your individual taste.</i>

9
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:29,804
<i>Hurry now, while stocks last, to your
nearest Central Services showroom.</i>

10
00:01:30,805 --> 00:01:34,600
<i>Designer colors
to suit your demanding taste.</i>

11
00:01:55,329 --> 00:01:58,040
<i>We have with us
from the Ministry of Information</i>

12
00:01:58,124 --> 00:02:00,918
<i>Deputy Minister, Eugene Helpmann.</i>

13
00:02:01,127 --> 00:02:03,004
<i>Good evening, David.</i>

14
00:02:03,337 --> 00:02:07,508
<i>What do you believe is behind
this recent increase in terrorist bombings?</i>

15
00:02:07,717 --> 00:02:09,510
<i>Bad sportsmanship.</i>

16
00:02:09,885 --> 00:02:12,013
<i>A ruthless minority of people</i>

17
00:02:12,096 --> 00:02:15,975
<i>seems to have forgotten
certain good old-fashioned virtues.</i>

18
00:02:16,309 --> 00:02:19,687
<i>They just can't stand seeing
the other fellow win.</i>

19
00:02:19,812 --> 00:02:22,315
<i>If these people would just play the game</i>

20
00:02:22,565 --> 00:02:24,400
<i>they'd get a lot more out of life.</i>

21
00:02:24,483 --> 00:02:27,403
<i>Nevertheless, Mr. Helpmann,
there are those who maintain</i>

22
00:02:27,486 --> 00:02:31,198
<i>that the Ministry of Information
has become too large and unwieldy.</i>

23
00:02:31,282 --> 00:02:33,993
<i>In free society,
information is the name of the game.</i>

24
00:02:34,076 --> 00:02:36,829
<i>You can't win the game
if you're a man short.</i>

25
00:02:36,912 --> 00:02:41,375
<i>The cost of it all, seven percent
of the gross national product.</i>

26
00:02:41,459 --> 00:02:44,920
<i>I understand this concern
on behalf of the taxpayers.</i>

27
00:02:45,004 --> 00:02:48,382
<i>People want value for money.
That is why we always insist</i>

28
00:02:48,466 --> 00:02:52,011
<i>on the principle
of Information Retrieval Charges.</i>

29
00:02:53,346 --> 00:02:55,264
<i>It's absolutely right and fair</i>

30
00:02:55,348 --> 00:02:58,768
<i>that those found guilty
should pay for their periods of detention</i>

31
00:02:59,310 --> 00:03:03,731
<i>and for the information retrieval
procedures used in their interrogation.</i>

32
00:03:04,148 --> 00:03:07,401
<i>Do you believe that the government
is winning against terrorists?</i>

33
00:03:07,485 --> 00:03:08,819
<i>Oh, yes.</i>

34
00:03:11,238 --> 00:03:14,325
<i>We're fielding all their strokes,
running all of them out</i>

35
00:03:14,408 --> 00:03:17,286
<i>and pretty consistently
knocking them for six.</i>

36
00:03:20,081 --> 00:03:21,999
<i>I'd say they're nearly out of the game.</i>

37
00:03:33,969 --> 00:03:37,556
<i>Mr. Helpmann, the bombing campaign
is now in its 13th year.</i>

38
00:03:38,265 --> 00:03:39,975
<i>Beginner's luck.</i>

39
00:03:41,227 --> 00:03:43,104
<i>Thank you very much, Deputy Minister.</i>

40
00:03:43,604 --> 00:03:47,733
<i>Thank you, David.
And a very merry Christmas to you all.</i>

41
00:03:50,403 --> 00:03:52,696
"Scrooge was better than his word.

42
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,741
"He did it all, and infinitely more.

43
00:03:55,991 --> 00:03:59,036
"And to Tiny Tim, who did not die

44
00:03:59,412 --> 00:04:01,997
"he became a second father.

45
00:04:02,248 --> 00:04:06,293
"He became as good a friend,
in the good old world.

46
00:04:07,002 --> 00:04:09,630
"And so, as Tiny Tim observed

47
00:04:10,172 --> 00:04:12,842
"God bless us, everyone."

48
00:04:17,555 --> 00:04:19,306
<i>Are you boys giving me the runaround?</i>

49
00:04:19,390 --> 00:04:22,351
<i>Now, I have to say, "Hey, hey."</i>

50
00:04:22,768 --> 00:04:25,354
<i>Don't throw that.
That's only for long distance.</i>

51
00:04:26,772 --> 00:04:29,358
<i>- I'm sorry, we haven't got any vacancies.
- No vacancies?</i>

52
00:04:29,442 --> 00:04:30,860
<i>We've got plenty of rooms.</i>

53
00:04:43,122 --> 00:04:44,623
Who's there?

54
00:04:47,751 --> 00:04:49,253
Who is that?

55
00:04:50,087 --> 00:04:53,883
Father Christmas can't come
if we haven't got a chimney.

56
00:04:53,966 --> 00:04:55,426
You'll see.

57
00:05:45,267 --> 00:05:47,853
"I hereby inform you,
under powers entrusted to me

58
00:05:47,937 --> 00:05:51,941
"under Section 47, Paragraph 7
of Council Order Number 438476

59
00:05:52,024 --> 00:05:56,111
"that Mr. Buttle, Archibald, residing
in 412 North Tower, Shangri-La Towers

60
00:05:56,195 --> 00:05:59,323
"is invited to assist
the Ministry of Information with inquiries.

61
00:05:59,448 --> 00:06:03,077
"And that he is liable
to financial obligations as specified in

62
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:07,206
"Council Order RB/CZ/907/X."

63
00:06:07,289 --> 00:06:08,374
Sign here, please.

64
00:06:11,710 --> 00:06:13,837
Where are you taking him?

65
00:06:14,463 --> 00:06:15,714
There.

66
00:06:17,633 --> 00:06:19,009
Thank you.

67
00:06:19,468 --> 00:06:20,970
Same again, please.

68
00:06:22,054 --> 00:06:24,390
Press harder this time. Good.

69
00:06:25,224 --> 00:06:27,059
What is this all about?

70
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:30,771
That is your receipt for your husband.
Thank you.

71
00:06:30,854 --> 00:06:33,482
This is my receipt for your receipt.

72
00:06:33,566 --> 00:06:35,317
Mrs. Buttle, are you okay?

73
00:06:39,363 --> 00:06:42,825
We're Department of Works!
Department of Works up here.

74
00:06:43,659 --> 00:06:46,579
- Careful with those bloody things.
- Sure.

75
00:06:47,204 --> 00:06:49,081
Don't take any notice of them.

76
00:06:49,164 --> 00:06:51,584
Don't touch me!
Why don't you just get out of here.

77
00:06:51,667 --> 00:06:54,670
Don't you worry.
We'll have everything shipshape in a jiffy.

78
00:06:55,129 --> 00:06:56,338
Nothing to worry about.

79
00:06:56,422 --> 00:06:59,008
It's Tuttle downstairs who can worry.

80
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:02,344
Tuttle? His name's Buttle.
There must be some mistake.

81
00:07:02,678 --> 00:07:05,556
Mistake? We don't make mistakes.

82
00:07:08,517 --> 00:07:12,563
Bloody typical. They've gone back
to metric without telling us.

83
00:07:12,688 --> 00:07:15,024
Mrs. Buttle, are you all right?

84
00:07:15,107 --> 00:07:16,400
Yes...

85
00:08:47,449 --> 00:08:48,617
Buttle.

86
00:08:51,787 --> 00:08:53,956
Buttle, Archibald.

87
00:09:02,798 --> 00:09:04,133
Oh, shit!

88
00:09:06,385 --> 00:09:07,803
Mister...

89
00:09:10,472 --> 00:09:13,392
Mr. Lowry, would you step in here, please.

90
00:09:49,845 --> 00:09:51,847
Has anybody seen Lowry?

91
00:09:54,850 --> 00:09:57,644
Has anybody seen

92
00:09:57,728 --> 00:09:59,646
Sam Lowry?

93
00:10:35,724 --> 00:10:36,892
Sam.

94
00:11:13,971 --> 00:11:15,848
<i>Hello? Sam. Where are you?</i>

95
00:11:15,931 --> 00:11:17,182
- Hello?
<i>- It's 11:00.</i>

96
00:11:18,183 --> 00:11:20,185
- What?
<i>- It's 11:00.</i>

97
00:11:20,435 --> 00:11:22,020
Mr. Kurtzmann.

98
00:11:23,814 --> 00:11:25,440
You're up late.

99
00:11:26,191 --> 00:11:27,526
Is it?

100
00:11:33,031 --> 00:11:36,326
- The electrics here are up the spout.
<i>- Yeah, I know.</i>

101
00:11:36,451 --> 00:11:37,953
- Yours, too?
<i>- Yes.</i>

102
00:11:39,454 --> 00:11:42,499
I'll be there as soon as I can.
Yes, thanks. 'Bye.

103
00:12:44,895 --> 00:12:47,022
This is not the only statue.

104
00:12:48,482 --> 00:12:50,609
What's this gun for?

105
00:12:50,692 --> 00:12:54,279
This one, Sister,
is a nine millimeter submachine gun

106
00:12:54,363 --> 00:12:57,991
used for close-quarter combat fighting.
Very effective weapon.

107
00:12:58,075 --> 00:12:59,076
Would you like to see it?

108
00:13:29,815 --> 00:13:30,983
Sam!

109
00:13:32,776 --> 00:13:33,986
Jack!

110
00:13:36,321 --> 00:13:38,073
Long time no see.

111
00:13:39,574 --> 00:13:40,867
What are you doing here?

112
00:13:40,951 --> 00:13:43,453
Didn't expect to see you
slumming it in Records.

113
00:13:43,578 --> 00:13:45,664
- What's the problem?
- Problem? No problem.

114
00:13:45,747 --> 00:13:47,332
Everything's fine. Marvelous.

115
00:13:47,666 --> 00:13:50,919
Alison's in great shape, kids are fine.
I'm on Security Level 5

116
00:13:51,003 --> 00:13:54,214
so Mr. Helpmann relies on me
more and more. It's been a great year.

117
00:13:54,756 --> 00:13:55,924
You sound worried.

118
00:13:56,133 --> 00:13:59,011
Worried? Me?
If I'm worried about anyone, Sam, it's you.

119
00:14:01,013 --> 00:14:02,431
Sam, whatever happened to you?

120
00:14:02,931 --> 00:14:04,266
Records.

121
00:14:16,528 --> 00:14:17,779
Sam?

122
00:14:20,323 --> 00:14:23,702
Jack, it's great to see you.
I'm going to be late.

123
00:14:23,952 --> 00:14:27,164
Sam, let a friend tell you
your life is going wrong.

124
00:14:27,289 --> 00:14:30,584
Records is a dead-end department.
No security that's worth a damn.

125
00:14:30,667 --> 00:14:34,004
- It's impossible to get noticed.
- I know. Wonderful.

126
00:14:34,129 --> 00:14:36,465
Marvelous. Perfect.

127
00:14:36,548 --> 00:14:39,760
See you, Jack.
Give my regards to Alison and the twins.

128
00:14:39,843 --> 00:14:41,720
- Triplets.
- Triplets?

129
00:14:41,928 --> 00:14:44,598
God, how time flies.

130
00:14:46,308 --> 00:14:50,145
A very clear delineation of picture, is it not?
Mobile.

131
00:14:50,645 --> 00:14:51,813
Next!

132
00:14:56,651 --> 00:14:59,321
I want to report a wrongful arrest.

133
00:14:59,696 --> 00:15:02,783
You want Information Adjustments.
Different department.

134
00:15:02,866 --> 00:15:05,911
I've been to Information Adjustments.
They sent me here.

135
00:15:05,994 --> 00:15:08,038
They said you have a form I need to fill out.

136
00:15:08,747 --> 00:15:11,541
- Have you got an arrest receipt?
- Yeah.

137
00:15:13,335 --> 00:15:14,669
Is it stamped?

138
00:15:15,504 --> 00:15:16,838
Stamped?

139
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:21,510
No, there's no stamp on it, you see.

140
00:15:22,135 --> 00:15:25,347
I can't let you have the form
until this is stamped.

141
00:15:25,472 --> 00:15:26,723
Where do I get it stamped?

142
00:15:27,265 --> 00:15:29,351
Information Adjustments.

143
00:15:42,239 --> 00:15:43,365
It's nothing.

144
00:15:46,576 --> 00:15:49,996
It keeps picking up old films.
That can't be right, can it?

145
00:15:50,539 --> 00:15:52,791
Perhaps the machine's on the blink.

146
00:15:52,874 --> 00:15:53,917
No, it's not the machine.

147
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,294
There's a mismatch
on the personnel code numbers.

148
00:15:56,711 --> 00:16:01,216
Tuttle should've had £31.06
debited against his account, not Buttle.

149
00:16:01,341 --> 00:16:04,219
- My God, a mistake!
- At least it's not ours.

150
00:16:04,553 --> 00:16:06,513
Isn't it? Whose is it?

151
00:16:06,596 --> 00:16:09,099
- Information Retrieval.
- Good.

152
00:16:09,224 --> 00:16:12,477
Expediting has put in
for electrical procedures in respect of

153
00:16:12,561 --> 00:16:15,355
Buttle, Archibald, shoe-repair operative.

154
00:16:15,438 --> 00:16:19,609
But Security has invoiced Admin.
for Tuttle, Archibald, heating engineer.

155
00:16:19,776 --> 00:16:22,737
What a relief. Well, finish your tea, Sam.

156
00:16:23,947 --> 00:16:25,240
Thanks.

157
00:16:26,408 --> 00:16:29,327
I don't know what I'd do
if you ever got promoted.

158
00:16:29,411 --> 00:16:33,456
- God, don't worry about that.
- But if they did promote you.

159
00:16:34,875 --> 00:16:37,544
I've told you before. I'd turn it down.

160
00:16:37,627 --> 00:16:39,880
- Would you, really?
- Really.

161
00:16:41,089 --> 00:16:42,090
You've been promoted.

162
00:16:46,761 --> 00:16:50,098
It's your mother, isn't it?
Pulling strings again.

163
00:16:54,603 --> 00:16:58,607
Sam, it's time for you to grow up
and accept responsibility.

164
00:16:58,773 --> 00:17:02,777
Your poor father would be appalled
at your lack of promotion.

165
00:17:03,612 --> 00:17:06,823
Mother, I just wish
you would stop interfering.

166
00:17:07,282 --> 00:17:10,535
I don't want promotion.
I'm happy where I am.

167
00:17:10,619 --> 00:17:13,955
No, you're not. Jack Lint is a lesson to you.

168
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:17,209
He doesn't have your brains,
but he's got the ambition.

169
00:17:17,292 --> 00:17:22,172
You haven't got the ambition. Luckily,
you've got me and the Deputy Minister.

170
00:17:22,297 --> 00:17:24,299
Mr. Helpmann was very close
to your father.

171
00:17:24,382 --> 00:17:27,594
Now please, Mrs. Lowry. Don't get upset.

172
00:17:27,677 --> 00:17:30,722
Mr. Lowry, please wait in the reception.
You're giving her wrinkles.

173
00:17:30,805 --> 00:17:32,557
- You see?
- God!

174
00:17:32,641 --> 00:17:34,768
Just try and relax, Mrs. Lowry.

175
00:17:35,352 --> 00:17:37,187
I'll make you 20 years younger.

176
00:17:37,979 --> 00:17:40,523
Dr. Jaffe, you are a genius.

177
00:17:40,649 --> 00:17:43,068
Would you like to be Surgeon General?

178
00:17:43,151 --> 00:17:45,862
I know simply everybody.

179
00:17:45,987 --> 00:17:49,282
They won't know you
when I've finished with you.

180
00:17:50,533 --> 00:17:51,868
First,

181
00:17:52,118 --> 00:17:54,329
we remove the excess derma.

182
00:17:57,290 --> 00:18:01,169
Now the flaccid tissues under the eyes
and forehead.

183
00:18:05,340 --> 00:18:07,884
Now, I lift

184
00:18:08,843 --> 00:18:11,263
the wrinkles and the worry lines

185
00:18:11,346 --> 00:18:14,349
right up into the wig, into the hairline.

186
00:18:16,851 --> 00:18:18,478
Now the template.

187
00:18:20,397 --> 00:18:23,775
Now, a bit of sticky.<i> Comme ça!</i>

188
00:18:24,192 --> 00:18:27,570
Already she's twice as beautiful
as she was before.

189
00:18:27,654 --> 00:18:28,655
<i>Voilà!</i>

190
00:18:30,490 --> 00:18:33,201
Mr. Helpmann
was very close to your poor father.

191
00:18:33,326 --> 00:18:36,663
He was close to me. He still is.

192
00:18:38,540 --> 00:18:42,711
He'll take you under his wing
when you get to Information Retrieval.

193
00:18:42,836 --> 00:18:44,713
You'll like it there.

194
00:18:44,879 --> 00:18:47,132
You're not listening to me, Mother.

195
00:18:51,720 --> 00:18:53,555
What the hell is this, lady?

196
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:56,558
It's a present for my son. Really!

197
00:18:57,392 --> 00:19:00,353
I hope you like it. It's very exclusive.

198
00:19:02,439 --> 00:19:04,065
What on Earth is it?

199
00:19:04,190 --> 00:19:07,068
Something for an executive.

200
00:19:10,572 --> 00:19:14,617
Madam Lowry! You look exquisite today.
Merry Christmas.

201
00:19:14,743 --> 00:19:17,078
Hello, Spiro. Merry Christmas.

202
00:19:17,704 --> 00:19:20,540
- You remember my son, Samuel?
- Of course.

203
00:19:20,623 --> 00:19:22,459
We're joining Mrs. Terrain.

204
00:19:22,584 --> 00:19:24,294
Yes, they're waiting.

205
00:19:31,301 --> 00:19:34,763
- Mother, I thought we'd be able to talk.
- We will.

206
00:19:34,888 --> 00:19:38,391
- Ida!
- God. She's got what's-her-name with her.

207
00:19:40,602 --> 00:19:43,396
Well, Alma, how are you?

208
00:19:43,480 --> 00:19:45,648
You look wonderful.

209
00:19:45,940 --> 00:19:47,817
Hello, Shirley.

210
00:19:48,443 --> 00:19:50,153
Thank you, Spiro.

211
00:19:51,071 --> 00:19:52,655
- Salt?
- Not yet.

212
00:19:53,281 --> 00:19:55,950
Sam, Happy Christmas.

213
00:19:57,994 --> 00:20:00,330
- So sorry we're late.
- Thanks.

214
00:20:00,455 --> 00:20:02,290
Now, Alma

215
00:20:02,749 --> 00:20:04,376
what are you going to have?

216
00:20:04,459 --> 00:20:08,046
I can't make up my mind whether to have
the number one or the number two.

217
00:20:08,129 --> 00:20:09,798
What do you recommend, Spiro?

218
00:20:09,923 --> 00:20:12,008
Between you and me, <i>madame,</i>

219
00:20:12,133 --> 00:20:14,928
-today the number two.
- Thank you, Spiro.

220
00:20:15,011 --> 00:20:17,180
Shirley, what are you going to have?

221
00:20:17,305 --> 00:20:21,351
Between you and me, <i>mademoiselle,
</i>today the number one.

222
00:20:21,684 --> 00:20:23,269
Madam Lowry?

223
00:20:23,520 --> 00:20:27,774
To hell with the diet!
I'm going to have a number eight, please.

224
00:20:27,857 --> 00:20:30,944
- A most perceptive choice, if I may say so.
- Oh, Ida.

225
00:20:31,027 --> 00:20:33,196
<i>- Monsieur?
</i>-Steak, please, rare. Mother...

226
00:20:33,321 --> 00:20:35,949
<i>- Monsieur, quel numéro?
</i>-I don't know which number.

227
00:20:36,032 --> 00:20:38,159
- Mother, please...
- That one. There.

228
00:20:38,284 --> 00:20:39,744
Thanks. Mother, will you...

229
00:20:39,828 --> 00:20:43,915
Alma! You wicked girl.
You've started your treatments.

230
00:20:43,998 --> 00:20:46,668
- Say the number, please.
- You noticed.

231
00:20:46,835 --> 00:20:49,170
- You have to say the number!
- Go away!

232
00:20:49,295 --> 00:20:51,131
I must tell you all about it.

233
00:20:51,214 --> 00:20:52,799
Number three.

234
00:20:55,009 --> 00:20:58,805
- Mother, will you listen to me?
<i>- Numéro huit.</i>

235
00:20:59,973 --> 00:21:04,060
Braised veal in wine sauce.

236
00:21:06,563 --> 00:21:11,317
It's too exciting.
I've left Dr. Jaffe and gone to Dr. Chapman.

237
00:21:11,401 --> 00:21:13,695
<i>Numéro deux,</i> duck <i>à l'orange.</i>

238
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:15,947
- Chapman?
<i>- Excusez-moi.</i>

239
00:21:16,030 --> 00:21:18,324
The acid man?

240
00:21:18,575 --> 00:21:22,162
Really, Ida. Just because
his techniques are revolutionary.

241
00:21:22,245 --> 00:21:25,373
I don't go around calling
Dr. Jaffe "the knife man."

242
00:21:25,498 --> 00:21:28,209
<i>Numéro un, crevettes à la mayonnaise.</i>

243
00:21:28,334 --> 00:21:29,961
I am sorry, Alma.

244
00:21:30,044 --> 00:21:32,964
- I didn't mean to...
- It's all right, Ida.

245
00:21:33,047 --> 00:21:35,758
Steak.<i> Numéro trois!</i>

246
00:21:38,052 --> 00:21:39,888
<i>Mesdames, monsieur</i>

247
00:21:40,597 --> 00:21:42,765
<i>bon appétit!</i>

248
00:21:42,891 --> 00:21:44,684
<i>- Merci!
- Merci!</i>

249
00:21:44,767 --> 00:21:49,481
Acid, on the other hand, can be used
for such wonderfully subtle shading

250
00:21:49,564 --> 00:21:51,649
such delicate nuances.

251
00:21:51,733 --> 00:21:55,153
Like a Rembrandt etching.
And it is so much quicker.

252
00:21:55,236 --> 00:21:59,199
If it wasn't for this complication,
it could've happened to anybody,

253
00:21:59,282 --> 00:22:02,410
I'd have had this dressing off yesterday.

254
00:22:03,203 --> 00:22:04,454
Shirley.

255
00:22:05,580 --> 00:22:06,581
Salt?

256
00:22:30,396 --> 00:22:31,731
What were we saying?

257
00:22:31,814 --> 00:22:33,733
God, this isn't rare.

258
00:22:34,150 --> 00:22:39,113
By the by, I saw the most wonderful idea
for Christmas presents at the chemist's.

259
00:22:39,239 --> 00:22:41,741
Gift tokens. Medical gift tokens.

260
00:22:41,824 --> 00:22:43,743
That sounds marvelous!

261
00:22:43,826 --> 00:22:47,580
It's good at any doctor's
and most of the major hospitals.

262
00:22:47,664 --> 00:22:51,376
It's also accepted
for gynecological examinations

263
00:22:51,459 --> 00:22:53,795
including Caesarian sections.

264
00:22:54,170 --> 00:22:56,339
- Spiro.
- Madam, I don't know what to say.

265
00:22:56,464 --> 00:23:00,635
This never happens to us. I'm going
to do something about it straightaway.

266
00:23:01,469 --> 00:23:04,806
Sam, can't you do something
about these terrorists?

267
00:23:05,265 --> 00:23:07,016
It's my lunch hour.

268
00:23:08,309 --> 00:23:10,144
Besides, it's not my department.

269
00:23:10,270 --> 00:23:15,024
Actually, Alma, that's one of
the little things I've been dying to tell you.

270
00:23:15,483 --> 00:23:19,445
Sam's being promoted
to Information Retrieval.

271
00:23:19,529 --> 00:23:23,616
Why, that's wonderful!
Congratulations, Sam.

272
00:23:23,825 --> 00:23:28,371
Stop it! I'm not being promoted.
I'm not going to Information Retrieval.

273
00:23:28,496 --> 00:23:31,499
If I wanted to upset you, Mother,
I'd tell you where to stick it.

274
00:23:35,628 --> 00:23:36,879
Pepper?

275
00:23:39,132 --> 00:23:40,133
I've got to go.

276
00:23:41,009 --> 00:23:43,094
You haven't had your dessert.

277
00:23:43,177 --> 00:23:45,888
I don't want dessert,
promotion or anything!

278
00:23:46,014 --> 00:23:50,351
Of course you want something.
You must have hopes, wishes, dreams?

279
00:23:50,560 --> 00:23:52,729
No, nothing. Not even dreams.

280
00:24:03,865 --> 00:24:05,033
Sam.

281
00:24:37,815 --> 00:24:38,983
Sam!

282
00:25:20,066 --> 00:25:21,401
Oh, God!

283
00:25:44,465 --> 00:25:46,801
Hello. Central Services?

284
00:25:47,218 --> 00:25:50,054
I'm at 579B, Block 19,
Northwestern Section D.

285
00:25:50,138 --> 00:25:53,391
Exit 1 on Green Pastures Highway
at the Orange Blossom Flyover.

286
00:25:53,474 --> 00:25:56,728
I've got trouble with my air conditioning.
It's an emergency.

287
00:25:56,811 --> 00:26:00,815
<i>Thank you for calling Central Services.
I'm sorry, due to staff shortages</i>

288
00:26:00,940 --> 00:26:05,319
<i>Central Services cannot take service calls
between 2300 and 0900 hours.</i>

289
00:26:05,445 --> 00:26:07,822
<i>Have a nice day.
This has not been a recording.</i>

290
00:26:07,905 --> 00:26:09,574
This is an emergency!

291
00:26:09,657 --> 00:26:14,328
<i>- Thank you for calling Central Services.</i>
- Look, I've got to have a heating engineer.

292
00:26:14,454 --> 00:26:17,331
<i>- Thank you for calling Central Services.</i>
- Oh, shit!

293
00:26:42,106 --> 00:26:43,357
Hello.

294
00:26:53,034 --> 00:26:54,827
- Hello.
<i>- Hello.</i>

295
00:27:04,545 --> 00:27:06,297
<i>Hello, Mr. Lowry.</i>

296
00:27:06,380 --> 00:27:08,132
Yes, who is this?

297
00:27:08,716 --> 00:27:11,719
<i>Put the phone down and raise your hands.</i>

298
00:27:12,136 --> 00:27:13,888
What? Who is this?

299
00:27:17,558 --> 00:27:19,393
Nice and easy, now.

300
00:27:25,483 --> 00:27:28,402
Just keep your hands where I can see them.

301
00:27:53,845 --> 00:27:55,429
What are you doing?

302
00:27:59,725 --> 00:28:03,020
Harry Tuttle, heating engineer,
at your service.

303
00:28:06,774 --> 00:28:08,067
Tuttle?

304
00:28:09,861 --> 00:28:11,404
Are you from Central Services?

305
00:28:17,618 --> 00:28:19,287
I called Central Services.

306
00:28:19,412 --> 00:28:20,788
They're overworked these days.

307
00:28:21,372 --> 00:28:23,916
Luckily, I intercepted your call.

308
00:28:24,792 --> 00:28:26,252
You did...

309
00:28:27,795 --> 00:28:30,798
Just a minute.
What was that business with the gun?

310
00:28:31,591 --> 00:28:33,593
Just a precaution, sir.

311
00:28:35,219 --> 00:28:37,972
I've had traps set for me before now.

312
00:28:38,431 --> 00:28:43,394
Plenty of people in Central Services would
love to get their hands on Harry Tuttle.

313
00:28:45,271 --> 00:28:47,273
Are you telling me that this is illegal?

314
00:28:54,071 --> 00:28:55,364
Well, yes and no.

315
00:28:55,448 --> 00:28:57,992
Officially, only Central Services
can touch this stuff.

316
00:28:58,075 --> 00:28:59,577
Would you hold this, please?

317
00:28:59,660 --> 00:29:02,413
But nowadays,
with all the new rules and regulations

318
00:29:02,496 --> 00:29:04,498
they can't get decent staff anymore.

319
00:29:04,582 --> 00:29:07,168
They turn a blind eye as long as I'm careful.

320
00:29:07,293 --> 00:29:10,671
But if they could prove that I've been
working on their equipment

321
00:29:10,796 --> 00:29:13,007
that's a plate of a different color.

322
00:29:13,090 --> 00:29:15,760
- Wouldn't it be simpler just to...
- Hold this, please.

323
00:29:15,843 --> 00:29:17,178
Sorry, yes.

324
00:29:17,303 --> 00:29:20,765
I was saying, wouldn't it be simpler
to work with Central Services?

325
00:29:20,848 --> 00:29:22,808
I couldn't stand the...

326
00:29:24,268 --> 00:29:26,687
- It's getting warm.
- You couldn't stand the what?

327
00:29:26,812 --> 00:29:28,689
I couldn't stand the paperwork.

328
00:29:28,773 --> 00:29:30,191
Listen,

329
00:29:30,274 --> 00:29:33,611
this system of yours could be on fire,
and I couldn't turn on a kitchen tap

330
00:29:33,694 --> 00:29:36,405
without filling out a 27B/6.

331
00:29:36,864 --> 00:29:38,616
Bloody paperwork.

332
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:41,535
I suppose one has to expect
a certain amount.

333
00:29:41,702 --> 00:29:44,455
Why? I came into this
for the action, the excitement.

334
00:29:44,538 --> 00:29:49,043
Go anywhere, travel light. Get in, get out,
wherever there's trouble. A man alone.

335
00:29:50,378 --> 00:29:51,754
They've got the whole country
sectioned off.

336
00:29:51,837 --> 00:29:53,798
Can't make a move without a form.

337
00:30:10,690 --> 00:30:13,067
Found it. There's your problem.

338
00:30:13,359 --> 00:30:15,319
- Can you fix it?
- No, I can't.

339
00:30:15,403 --> 00:30:19,073
But I can bypass it with one of these.

340
00:30:21,534 --> 00:30:22,994
Fine by me.

341
00:30:27,915 --> 00:30:29,750
- Are you expecting anyone?
- No.

342
00:30:35,423 --> 00:30:37,174
Wait here. Wait.

343
00:30:43,848 --> 00:30:46,267
The flashlight.

344
00:30:49,437 --> 00:30:51,355
- Central Services.
- Central Services.

345
00:30:51,439 --> 00:30:54,442
- Yes.
- You rang, sir.

346
00:30:54,567 --> 00:30:57,111
Trouble with your air conditioning?

347
00:30:57,278 --> 00:30:58,779
No, it's all right.

348
00:31:01,782 --> 00:31:02,867
It's fixed.

349
00:31:03,284 --> 00:31:04,618
It's fixed?

350
00:31:05,036 --> 00:31:08,748
Yes. I mean, it fixed itself.

351
00:31:09,040 --> 00:31:10,708
Fixed itself, did it?

352
00:31:11,751 --> 00:31:12,877
Yes.

353
00:31:14,628 --> 00:31:16,964
Machines don't fix themselves.

354
00:31:17,965 --> 00:31:20,134
He's tampered with it.

355
00:31:21,802 --> 00:31:24,221
Sorry you had a wasted journey.

356
00:31:25,598 --> 00:31:28,059
I think we ought to have a look.

357
00:31:30,936 --> 00:31:32,897
Have you got a 27B/6?

358
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:34,315
A 27B/6?

359
00:31:42,490 --> 00:31:43,991
Now look what you've done to him.

360
00:31:46,243 --> 00:31:48,162
Have you got a 27B/6?

361
00:31:48,579 --> 00:31:50,581
Not as such.

362
00:31:52,625 --> 00:31:54,418
But we'll get one.

363
00:31:55,836 --> 00:31:57,755
It's all right, it's all right.

364
00:31:58,297 --> 00:32:01,342
Sorry. I'm a bit of a stickler for paperwork.

365
00:32:01,425 --> 00:32:04,345
Where would we be
if we didn't stick to the correct procedures?

366
00:32:04,428 --> 00:32:06,013
We'll be back.

367
00:32:07,014 --> 00:32:08,557
We'll be back!

368
00:32:14,021 --> 00:32:15,272
Thanks for coming.

369
00:32:15,356 --> 00:32:17,650
We'll be back, you clever bastard!

370
00:32:18,025 --> 00:32:20,444
- Thank you.
- We'll show you.

371
00:32:27,868 --> 00:32:31,372
Thanks, Lowry.
You're a good man in a tight corner.

372
00:32:40,548 --> 00:32:43,134
I just happen to work
at the Ministry of Information.

373
00:32:43,217 --> 00:32:44,218
What?

374
00:32:45,886 --> 00:32:48,222
I just happen to know that

375
00:32:48,722 --> 00:32:52,726
Information Retrieval
are looking for an Archibald Tuttle,

376
00:32:52,810 --> 00:32:54,562
heating engineer.

377
00:32:57,314 --> 00:33:00,067
You wouldn't by any chance... Would you?

378
00:33:02,528 --> 00:33:04,905
My good friends call me Harry.

379
00:33:06,740 --> 00:33:08,701
Information Retrieval?

380
00:33:09,410 --> 00:33:10,911
The big boys.

381
00:33:16,417 --> 00:33:18,627
What do they want you for?

382
00:33:23,674 --> 00:33:25,134
Time to go.

383
00:33:25,759 --> 00:33:27,219
How's that?

384
00:33:32,725 --> 00:33:35,519
You really must let me give you something.

385
00:33:39,565 --> 00:33:42,109
How much will it be for the work?

386
00:33:43,944 --> 00:33:47,615
Come on. You did me a favor.
Just check the corridor.

387
00:33:50,910 --> 00:33:52,161
Thanks.

388
00:33:52,244 --> 00:33:55,080
Listen, kid. We're all in this together.

389
00:33:55,247 --> 00:33:56,498
Go on.

390
00:34:10,721 --> 00:34:11,847
All clear.

391
00:34:40,834 --> 00:34:43,337
I think Kurtzmann is getting suspicious.

392
00:34:46,632 --> 00:34:48,550
What's playing today?

393
00:34:49,218 --> 00:34:50,678
<i>Casablanca.</i>

394
00:34:50,761 --> 00:34:53,222
<i>Mr. Lowry, step in here, please.</i>

395
00:34:57,101 --> 00:34:58,352
<i>Lowry!</i>

396
00:35:00,187 --> 00:35:02,189
<i>Now, Mr. Lowry!</i>

397
00:35:14,994 --> 00:35:18,247
Thank God you're here.
We're in terrible trouble.

398
00:35:26,338 --> 00:35:27,673
Look at this.

399
00:35:27,756 --> 00:35:30,759
- It's a check.
- It's the refund for Tuttle.

400
00:35:30,843 --> 00:35:32,136
Tuttle?

401
00:35:33,637 --> 00:35:35,597
Buttle. I mean Buttle.

402
00:35:35,973 --> 00:35:38,100
It's been confusion from the word go.

403
00:35:38,183 --> 00:35:41,103
He was overcharged
for Information Retrieval procedures.

404
00:35:41,186 --> 00:35:44,106
And someone, somewhere,
is trying to make us carry the can.

405
00:35:44,189 --> 00:35:47,818
Can I have a look?
I've never seen a refund check before.

406
00:35:47,901 --> 00:35:49,945
I'll bet it's Jeffries.

407
00:35:51,864 --> 00:35:53,032
Yes.

408
00:35:53,240 --> 00:35:56,410
He believes that people
should pay more for their interrogation.

409
00:35:56,493 --> 00:35:58,537
And B: he loathes me.

410
00:35:58,704 --> 00:36:00,456
We've got to get rid of it.

411
00:36:00,539 --> 00:36:03,792
Send it to somebody else.
Better still, send it to Buttle.

412
00:36:03,876 --> 00:36:05,461
After all, it's his check.

413
00:36:05,544 --> 00:36:07,880
I've tried that. Look at this.

414
00:36:08,130 --> 00:36:09,173
You see?

415
00:36:09,256 --> 00:36:12,426
The population census
has got him down as dormanted.

416
00:36:12,509 --> 00:36:16,096
The Central Collective Storehouse
computer has got him down as deleted.

417
00:36:16,180 --> 00:36:17,181
Hang on.

418
00:36:17,264 --> 00:36:19,641
Information Retrieval's
got him inoperative.

419
00:36:19,725 --> 00:36:22,394
Security has got him down as excised.

420
00:36:22,478 --> 00:36:25,230
- Administration's got him as completed.
- He's dead.

421
00:36:27,649 --> 00:36:28,817
Dead?

422
00:36:31,278 --> 00:36:33,197
Well, that's awful.

423
00:36:37,618 --> 00:36:40,579
We'll never get rid of the damn thing now.

424
00:36:41,288 --> 00:36:43,248
What are we going to do, Sam?

425
00:36:43,916 --> 00:36:45,459
Could try the next of kin.

426
00:36:53,467 --> 00:36:56,053
There we go. "Buttle, Veronica, Mrs."

427
00:36:56,136 --> 00:36:57,513
Veronica?

428
00:36:58,472 --> 00:37:00,766
What's the number on the check?

429
00:37:00,849 --> 00:37:04,144
27156789

430
00:37:06,271 --> 00:37:09,775
/074328K.

431
00:37:12,778 --> 00:37:14,571
Right into memory.

432
00:37:16,281 --> 00:37:17,950
Central Banking.

433
00:37:19,326 --> 00:37:21,120
Veronica Buttle

434
00:37:21,912 --> 00:37:23,080
Mrs.

435
00:37:24,206 --> 00:37:25,541
Deposit.

436
00:37:40,639 --> 00:37:42,433
Thank God for that.

437
00:37:42,683 --> 00:37:44,435
Would you like some tea?

438
00:37:54,695 --> 00:37:56,363
There's a problem.

439
00:37:56,447 --> 00:37:58,907
She doesn't have a bank account.

440
00:38:02,911 --> 00:38:04,830
Well, that's it then.

441
00:38:06,039 --> 00:38:09,084
I might just as well
go and hang myself now.

442
00:38:11,795 --> 00:38:16,341
This could never have happened
before the stupid 7th tier reorganization.

443
00:38:16,425 --> 00:38:18,051
That's Simmons' doing.

444
00:38:18,135 --> 00:38:20,554
He and Jeffries
always sit together at lunch.

445
00:38:20,637 --> 00:38:21,972
Bastards!

446
00:38:28,312 --> 00:38:32,441
Perhaps we could lose the thing
behind a filing cabinet or something

447
00:38:32,524 --> 00:38:35,986
-or burn it, eat it...
- Look. There is one solution.

448
00:38:36,069 --> 00:38:38,113
- What?
- Drive out to Mrs. Buttle

449
00:38:38,197 --> 00:38:41,658
let her sign at the back of the check.
She can cash it at the corner sweet shop.

450
00:38:43,785 --> 00:38:45,704
That's brilliant, Sam.

451
00:38:45,787 --> 00:38:47,372
I'll even do it for you.

452
00:38:47,456 --> 00:38:49,791
Here, authorize the check.
What's the address?

453
00:38:49,875 --> 00:38:51,793
Everything's in here.

454
00:38:55,297 --> 00:38:56,590
What shall I do next?

455
00:38:59,176 --> 00:39:00,594
Pink and blue receipts.

456
00:39:00,677 --> 00:39:04,306
Sign there, there and there.
And sign the check there.

457
00:39:10,103 --> 00:39:12,314
I think I've broken a bone.

458
00:39:12,648 --> 00:39:16,485
My wrist is all limp.
What a pathetic creature I am!

459
00:39:19,488 --> 00:39:21,323
I'll do it for you.

460
00:39:40,217 --> 00:39:42,344
Right. I'll be on my way.

461
00:39:45,222 --> 00:39:46,390
Sam?

462
00:39:49,810 --> 00:39:51,478
You are good to me, you know.

463
00:39:52,437 --> 00:39:55,524
<i>- Here's looking at you, kid.</i>
- Here's looking at you.

464
00:40:14,001 --> 00:40:17,045
<i>We interrupt this program
to bring you news of a terrorist bombing</i>

465
00:40:17,129 --> 00:40:18,672
<i>at the Blue Lagoon...</i>

466
00:40:59,671 --> 00:41:01,632
Hello. I wonder if you could tell me where...

467
00:41:01,715 --> 00:41:03,425
Piss off!

468
00:41:08,221 --> 00:41:09,473
Hello.

469
00:41:11,224 --> 00:41:13,060
I'll never confess!

470
00:41:19,024 --> 00:41:21,652
Come on, you've got to interrogate me.

471
00:41:47,803 --> 00:41:49,304
Mrs. Buttle?

472
00:41:53,767 --> 00:41:55,268
Mrs. Buttle?

473
00:42:04,194 --> 00:42:06,029
Are you Mrs. Buttle?

474
00:42:13,286 --> 00:42:15,038
My name is Lowry.

475
00:42:16,790 --> 00:42:18,208
Sam Lowry.

476
00:42:21,044 --> 00:42:23,755
I'm from the Ministry of Information.

477
00:42:26,758 --> 00:42:29,136
I've come to give you a check.

478
00:42:34,391 --> 00:42:37,519
It's a refund, actually. There was a mistake.

479
00:42:38,103 --> 00:42:39,438
Mistake?

480
00:42:39,521 --> 00:42:43,024
Yes. Not my department, of course.
I'm only Records.

481
00:42:45,152 --> 00:42:50,115
I'll tell you what it is. It seems Mr. Buttle
was overcharged by Information Retrieval.

482
00:42:50,991 --> 00:42:53,952
They don't usually make mistakes

483
00:42:54,619 --> 00:42:56,788
but I suppose we're all human.

484
00:43:01,501 --> 00:43:03,587
What happened to the...

485
00:43:08,008 --> 00:43:10,844
Actually, my coming here
is rather unorthodox.

486
00:43:10,927 --> 00:43:15,682
Bringing you this check. Any payments are
normally made through Central Computer

487
00:43:15,849 --> 00:43:18,143
but as there were certain

488
00:43:18,810 --> 00:43:22,314
difficulties, we thought,
rather than cause delay

489
00:43:22,898 --> 00:43:25,025
we'd let you have it now.

490
00:43:25,317 --> 00:43:27,611
It being Christmas and all.

491
00:43:28,612 --> 00:43:30,906
My husband's dead, isn't he?

492
00:43:33,617 --> 00:43:37,078
I do assure you, Mrs. Buttle,
the Ministry is very scrupulous

493
00:43:37,162 --> 00:43:39,956
about following up
and eradicating any error.

494
00:43:40,040 --> 00:43:42,751
But if you do have any complaints
you wish to make,

495
00:43:42,834 --> 00:43:46,963
I'd be only too happy
to send you the appropriate forms.

496
00:43:47,297 --> 00:43:49,841
What have you done with his body?

497
00:43:56,807 --> 00:44:00,268
I don't know anything about that.
I'm really just

498
00:44:00,352 --> 00:44:02,562
delivering the check. So if...

499
00:44:02,646 --> 00:44:06,650
Look, if you wouldn't mind
just signing these two receipts,

500
00:44:07,359 --> 00:44:11,363
I'd be only too happy to leave you in peace.

501
00:44:11,530 --> 00:44:13,615
He hadn't done anything.

502
00:44:14,032 --> 00:44:15,534
He was good!

503
00:44:17,160 --> 00:44:21,623
What have you done with his body?

504
00:44:28,797 --> 00:44:31,424
It isn't being very helpful, is it?

505
00:44:38,932 --> 00:44:42,269
I didn't have to come here,
you know, Mrs. Buttle.

506
00:44:43,603 --> 00:44:45,772
I'll kill you, you bastard!

507
00:44:47,649 --> 00:44:48,984
Stop it!

508
00:44:59,744 --> 00:45:01,538
Are you all right?

509
00:45:02,914 --> 00:45:04,708
Are you all right?

510
00:45:09,588 --> 00:45:11,506
Mrs. Buttle, are you all right?

511
00:45:11,590 --> 00:45:12,716
It's you.

512
00:45:13,717 --> 00:45:15,635
Wait, stop! Don't go!

513
00:46:28,416 --> 00:46:32,045
Stop! I had nothing to do with it. Wait!

514
00:46:32,128 --> 00:46:33,338
Stop!

515
00:46:56,569 --> 00:46:58,863
- Go away.
- Her name's Jill.

516
00:46:59,739 --> 00:47:00,907
What?

517
00:47:01,324 --> 00:47:02,492
Jill?

518
00:47:02,909 --> 00:47:04,244
Jill who?

519
00:47:04,744 --> 00:47:05,787
Jill who?

520
00:47:07,122 --> 00:47:08,248
Layton.

521
00:47:10,542 --> 00:47:12,002
Jill Layton.

522
00:47:13,211 --> 00:47:15,505
You're a very good little girl.

523
00:47:15,588 --> 00:47:18,591
- What are you doing here?
- Waiting for my daddy.

524
00:47:18,758 --> 00:47:21,469
He will be pleased when he gets home.

525
00:48:06,931 --> 00:48:10,226
Sam, I've just had
the transport pool onto me.

526
00:48:10,310 --> 00:48:13,730
You don't know anything about a personnel
transporter gone missing, do you?

527
00:48:13,813 --> 00:48:15,523
Personnel transporter?

528
00:48:15,940 --> 00:48:16,941
What?

529
00:48:17,567 --> 00:48:20,361
The pranets, I had a personal transporter.

530
00:48:20,445 --> 00:48:22,822
I'll do the paperwork in the morning.

531
00:48:24,199 --> 00:48:26,910
Is it all right about Mrs. Tuttle...

532
00:48:27,077 --> 00:48:28,995
Buttle's check?

533
00:48:30,080 --> 00:48:31,748
Can I forget it?

534
00:48:32,332 --> 00:48:35,251
What a relief. I'm gonna have nightmares.

535
00:48:38,463 --> 00:48:39,547
Damn! Blast!

536
00:48:40,799 --> 00:48:42,550
What's the matter?

537
00:48:43,134 --> 00:48:46,596
You don't happen to know
how to get round an IRQ/3, do you?

538
00:48:47,222 --> 00:48:49,766
All information
on third level suspects is classified.

539
00:48:49,974 --> 00:48:54,312
All inquiries through Information Retrieval.
That's hopeless, they'll tell you nothing.

540
00:48:54,395 --> 00:48:57,524
Come the time they want something
from us, yes.

541
00:48:57,732 --> 00:49:00,026
There's one way I could get round it.

542
00:49:00,193 --> 00:49:03,988
- I could accept my mother's promotion.
- Yes.

543
00:49:04,239 --> 00:49:05,323
No!

544
00:49:06,950 --> 00:49:08,701
You can't. You turned it down.

545
00:49:09,536 --> 00:49:10,954
I didn't sign the form.

546
00:49:12,747 --> 00:49:14,040
I did it for you.

547
00:49:15,500 --> 00:49:17,293
- You didn't?
- I did.

548
00:49:18,711 --> 00:49:19,712
You didn't!

549
00:49:20,630 --> 00:49:21,840
I did.

550
00:49:22,215 --> 00:49:25,969
- Why did you do that?
- That's what you wanted, isn't it?

551
00:49:27,512 --> 00:49:29,013
I don't know.

552
00:49:29,722 --> 00:49:31,808
I don't know what I want.

553
00:49:33,351 --> 00:49:35,895
Come on, before they turn the lights out.

554
00:49:52,954 --> 00:49:54,205
<i>There is one way around it.</i>

555
00:49:55,039 --> 00:49:58,376
<i>I could accept promotion
to Information Retrieval.</i>

556
00:52:16,931 --> 00:52:18,725
What are you doing?

557
00:52:18,975 --> 00:52:20,727
My God, what's going on?

558
00:52:20,810 --> 00:52:22,687
Emergency procedures.

559
00:52:24,188 --> 00:52:26,357
I haven't got an emergency.

560
00:52:26,441 --> 00:52:27,984
<i>This is an emergency.</i>

561
00:52:28,067 --> 00:52:30,820
<i>Have a nice day.
This has not been a recording.</i>

562
00:52:33,072 --> 00:52:34,741
Here. Sign that.

563
00:52:36,451 --> 00:52:37,660
What is it?

564
00:52:37,744 --> 00:52:41,122
It's a 27B/6. What do you think it is?
Where is he?

565
00:52:41,414 --> 00:52:43,750
Spoor, come here. I found it.

566
00:52:45,418 --> 00:52:47,128
Spoor, come here!

567
00:52:47,295 --> 00:52:50,840
Look there, a bypass valve
on the transformer.

568
00:53:03,644 --> 00:53:05,646
You've had that scab Tuttle here.

569
00:53:06,189 --> 00:53:07,190
What are you talking about?

570
00:53:08,483 --> 00:53:10,693
Who fixed your ducts?

571
00:53:11,736 --> 00:53:14,655
I told you before, they fixed themselves.

572
00:53:15,281 --> 00:53:17,742
Where did this come from,
out of your nostril?

573
00:53:23,539 --> 00:53:25,291
Central Services.

574
00:53:27,794 --> 00:53:30,380
They don't take kindly to sabotage.

575
00:53:34,550 --> 00:53:37,053
This place is in a terrible state.

576
00:53:38,012 --> 00:53:41,140
Just a minute. You're not gonna
leave it like this, are you?

577
00:53:41,391 --> 00:53:42,517
Why not?

578
00:53:42,600 --> 00:53:46,104
All you got to do is blow your nose
and it's fixed, isn't it?

579
00:54:24,684 --> 00:54:27,478
<i>What have you done with his body?</i>

580
00:57:46,302 --> 00:57:49,805
<i>Mrs. Ida Lowry requests the pleasure
of your company</i>

581
00:57:49,889 --> 00:57:52,850
<i>at her apartment tonight from 8:30</i>

582
00:57:55,978 --> 00:57:57,563
<i>to midnight</i>

583
00:57:58,064 --> 00:58:00,316
<i>To celebrate the completion</i>

584
00:58:00,399 --> 00:58:02,735
<i>of her recent cosmetic</i>

585
00:58:03,069 --> 00:58:05,446
<i>surgery</i>

586
00:58:25,174 --> 00:58:26,425
Thanks.

587
00:58:26,717 --> 00:58:28,386
It's reply paid.

588
00:58:28,719 --> 00:58:29,929
Is it?

589
00:58:34,350 --> 00:58:35,685
Two, three.

590
00:58:35,768 --> 00:58:39,105
<i>Mother, I'm sorry but I am unable
to come to your party</i>

591
00:58:39,230 --> 00:58:41,357
You don't have to sing it.

592
00:58:41,440 --> 00:58:42,775
I don't?

593
00:58:49,949 --> 00:58:52,785
Aren't you a bit late?
The party started an hour ago.

594
00:58:52,910 --> 00:58:55,663
Yes, it's backlog. Everybody complains.

595
00:58:56,288 --> 00:58:58,457
Was it all right otherwise?

596
00:59:07,591 --> 00:59:11,303
<i>Mr. Helpmann, I'm keen
to get into Information Retrieval.</i>

597
00:59:11,512 --> 00:59:14,640
<i>Mr. Helpmann,
I'm dying to get at this woman.</i>

598
00:59:24,316 --> 00:59:26,193
Where's your invitation?

599
00:59:26,318 --> 00:59:28,487
Don't shoot! Mother!

600
00:59:30,781 --> 00:59:34,160
Sam, I'm so glad you came.

601
00:59:35,453 --> 00:59:36,704
Come in.

602
00:59:37,455 --> 00:59:38,664
Mother?

603
00:59:41,000 --> 00:59:43,461
Oh, God, Mother, is that you?

604
00:59:43,544 --> 00:59:46,380
Of course. Isn't it wonderful?

605
00:59:46,797 --> 00:59:49,675
The bandages came off just this afternoon.

606
00:59:50,050 --> 00:59:52,303
Come on, darling, join in the fun.

607
00:59:52,386 --> 00:59:54,722
Simply everybody's here.

608
00:59:54,847 --> 00:59:57,641
- Is Mr. Helpmann here? Stop shoving!
- He is.

609
00:59:57,725 --> 01:00:01,020
Mr. Helpmann is here,
and he wants to talk to you.

610
01:00:01,145 --> 01:00:03,314
Good. I want to talk to him.

611
01:00:03,397 --> 01:00:07,234
He says you're the first person
ever to refuse a promotion.

612
01:00:07,359 --> 01:00:11,322
- He thinks you should see a doctor.
- Actually, Mother, I've decided...

613
01:00:12,072 --> 01:00:13,866
Louis, you know Sam.

614
01:00:14,700 --> 01:00:16,494
Can you believe it?

615
01:00:16,869 --> 01:00:19,371
It's just me and my little knife.

616
01:00:19,497 --> 01:00:20,748
Snip.

617
01:00:21,040 --> 01:00:22,249
Slice.

618
01:00:22,917 --> 01:00:25,628
- Can you believe it?
- Congratulations.

619
01:00:25,711 --> 01:00:28,297
- But this is just the beginning.
- Really?

620
01:00:28,380 --> 01:00:31,175
Of course, yes.
You've seen her with her clothes off.

621
01:00:31,258 --> 01:00:34,386
Faces are a doddle
compared to tits and ass.

622
01:00:34,470 --> 01:00:38,390
- No hairlines.
- Really, Louis!

623
01:00:38,891 --> 01:00:41,602
I've been looking for you everywhere.

624
01:00:46,232 --> 01:00:49,068
Mother, I have to talk to Mr. Helpmann.

625
01:00:49,568 --> 01:00:51,821
- You stupid sod.
- Dear boy

626
01:00:52,822 --> 01:00:55,407
what do you think of your mother now?

627
01:00:55,491 --> 01:00:57,243
It will never last.

628
01:00:59,703 --> 01:01:01,080
Excuse me.

629
01:01:01,247 --> 01:01:03,415
Dr. Chapman, did you say something?

630
01:01:03,541 --> 01:01:05,876
That technique, I've tried it.

631
01:01:06,585 --> 01:01:09,421
A nice effect, but highly unstable.

632
01:01:10,089 --> 01:01:13,259
In six months,
she'll look like Grandma Moses.

633
01:01:13,759 --> 01:01:15,594
See here, Chapman.

634
01:01:15,678 --> 01:01:19,598
At least mine don't end up looking
like they've been mugged.

635
01:01:24,603 --> 01:01:28,858
<i>What have you done with his body?</i>

636
01:01:33,404 --> 01:01:36,949
- What happened to you?
- There's a little complication.

637
01:01:37,032 --> 01:01:41,620
Dr. Chapman says it often happens
with a delicate skin like mine.

638
01:01:42,246 --> 01:01:44,123
Nothing to worry about.

639
01:01:44,248 --> 01:01:47,626
He's promised these bandages
will be off in a jiffy.

640
01:01:47,751 --> 01:01:51,130
- Actually, there's someone I want to meet.
- I know.

641
01:01:54,967 --> 01:01:56,427
Here we are.

642
01:01:58,053 --> 01:02:01,140
I'm going to leave you two lovebirds
in peace.

643
01:02:03,392 --> 01:02:05,144
Hello, Shirley.

644
01:02:07,146 --> 01:02:08,439
Come on.

645
01:02:09,064 --> 01:02:10,900
Do you want a drink?

646
01:02:17,156 --> 01:02:18,741
Chase those years away.

647
01:02:18,824 --> 01:02:20,492
Listen, Shirley.

648
01:02:21,076 --> 01:02:24,663
Look, your mother and my mother
have the idea that...

649
01:02:25,831 --> 01:02:27,416
Look, I'm very flattered.

650
01:02:27,499 --> 01:02:31,170
And, well, I just don't want you
to be under any false impressions.

651
01:02:31,253 --> 01:02:32,838
It's all right.

652
01:02:33,130 --> 01:02:35,174
I don't like you, either.

653
01:02:37,676 --> 01:02:38,761
Sam!

654
01:02:40,012 --> 01:02:42,473
Jack! Excuse me, Shirley.

655
01:02:43,807 --> 01:02:45,351
I'm sorry.

656
01:02:48,354 --> 01:02:50,356
- Do you remember Alison?
- Good evening.

657
01:02:50,481 --> 01:02:52,942
Alison, Sam Lowry, an old friend.

658
01:02:53,025 --> 01:02:55,027
Alison, yes, of course.

659
01:02:55,861 --> 01:02:58,364
- You look different.
- I'm two years older.

660
01:02:58,489 --> 01:03:01,617
And she's been to Dr. Jaffe.
She doesn't like me telling people.

661
01:03:01,700 --> 01:03:03,702
But, in fact, she's really pleased.

662
01:03:03,827 --> 01:03:07,623
- I knew there was something different.
- Remember how they used to stick out?

663
01:03:07,706 --> 01:03:08,874
What?

664
01:03:09,041 --> 01:03:11,377
Yes, well, rather.

665
01:03:11,961 --> 01:03:15,798
- I always used to wonder if they were real.
- What, my ears?

666
01:03:18,550 --> 01:03:21,720
- Dr. Jaffe pinned her ears back.
- Yes, that's...

667
01:03:22,638 --> 01:03:25,015
I always wondered if she wore falsies.

668
01:03:25,099 --> 01:03:26,600
Mr. Helpmann.

669
01:03:27,726 --> 01:03:29,103
False ears.

670
01:03:30,604 --> 01:03:33,065
- Hello, Jack.
- You remember my wife, Alison?

671
01:03:33,190 --> 01:03:36,568
Of course, Barbara, isn't it? How are you?

672
01:03:36,694 --> 01:03:39,196
Barbara's very well. How are you?

673
01:03:39,405 --> 01:03:40,906
- Fine, thanks.
- Good.

674
01:03:41,031 --> 01:03:45,119
Hello, Sam. Ida said you might be here.
Have you got a minute?

675
01:03:45,452 --> 01:03:47,287
Would you excuse us?

676
01:03:47,871 --> 01:03:50,708
Of course. Come on, Alison. Barbara.

677
01:03:52,876 --> 01:03:53,919
Sam,

678
01:03:58,257 --> 01:03:59,925
I need your help.

679
01:04:23,157 --> 01:04:24,742
Thanks very much.

680
01:04:24,825 --> 01:04:27,202
Mr. Helpmann, I'm glad to be of help.

681
01:04:27,286 --> 01:04:29,455
And if I can help you...

682
01:04:35,627 --> 01:04:38,130
Your father and I were very close.

683
01:04:38,672 --> 01:04:42,801
Of course, Jeremiah was senior to me,
but we were close friends.

684
01:04:43,010 --> 01:04:45,304
Especially after the bombing.

685
01:04:46,680 --> 01:04:50,309
And now I keep his name alive
at the office every day.

686
01:04:51,477 --> 01:04:54,271
It's as though he's there
still speaking to me.

687
01:04:54,605 --> 01:04:56,106
"'Ere I am

688
01:04:57,858 --> 01:04:59,026
"J.H."

689
01:05:01,987 --> 01:05:04,031
The ghost in the machine.

690
01:05:06,658 --> 01:05:09,495
I know he would've wanted me to help you.

691
01:05:12,164 --> 01:05:14,291
And I promised your mother

692
01:05:14,666 --> 01:05:18,462
that I'd take you onto the team
at Information Retrieval.

693
01:05:19,171 --> 01:05:21,840
- But I gather that you...
- Well,

694
01:05:22,549 --> 01:05:25,677
actually, Mr. Helpmann,
I've changed my mind.

695
01:05:26,470 --> 01:05:29,181
I've decided I'd accept the transfer.

696
01:05:30,015 --> 01:05:31,850
If I'm not too late.

697
01:05:32,893 --> 01:05:34,228
Too late?

698
01:05:34,478 --> 01:05:36,355
That's for me to say.

699
01:05:37,731 --> 01:05:39,525
Well, then I am...

700
01:05:42,319 --> 01:05:44,822
Welcome to Information Retrieval.

701
01:06:18,272 --> 01:06:19,940
My name's Lowry.

702
01:06:21,400 --> 01:06:22,734
Sam Lowry.

703
01:06:23,735 --> 01:06:26,613
I've been told to report to Mr. Warren.

704
01:06:27,239 --> 01:06:29,074
Thirtieth floor, sir.

705
01:06:29,241 --> 01:06:30,909
You're expected.

706
01:06:35,080 --> 01:06:37,833
- Don't you want to search me?
- No, sir.

707
01:06:40,252 --> 01:06:42,754
- Want to see my ID?
- No need, sir.

708
01:06:45,090 --> 01:06:48,302
- But I could be anybody.
- No, you couldn't, sir.

709
01:06:48,427 --> 01:06:50,762
This is Information Retrieval.

710
01:06:52,014 --> 01:06:54,099
The lift's arrived, sir.

711
01:08:42,249 --> 01:08:45,127
Half as terrorists, rest as victims.

712
01:08:46,128 --> 01:08:47,504
Definitely, no.

713
01:08:47,587 --> 01:08:50,090
My name's Lowry, Mr. Warren.
Sam Lowry.

714
01:08:50,173 --> 01:08:53,427
Yes. No. Cancel that. Okay.

715
01:08:54,720 --> 01:08:56,847
Glad to have you on board.

716
01:08:58,890 --> 01:09:01,184
Don't be ridiculous, Jenkins.

717
01:09:01,643 --> 01:09:02,769
Yes.

718
01:09:03,061 --> 01:09:05,439
You'll like it up here.

719
01:09:05,564 --> 01:09:09,026
We've got a crack team of...
Are they kidding?...decision makers.

720
01:09:09,109 --> 01:09:13,363
In triplicate. I'm expecting big things...
Two copies to Finance!...of you.

721
01:09:17,284 --> 01:09:19,202
Between you and me, Lowry,

722
01:09:19,453 --> 01:09:22,789
this department is about to be upgraded.

723
01:09:25,625 --> 01:09:27,044
Here we are.

724
01:09:27,252 --> 01:09:29,921
Your very own number
on your very own door.

725
01:09:30,130 --> 01:09:32,966
And behind that door, your very own office.

726
01:09:33,050 --> 01:09:36,219
Congratulations, DZ/015.

727
01:09:36,595 --> 01:09:38,221
Welcome to the team.

728
01:09:38,388 --> 01:09:40,724
Yes. No. Cancel that.

729
01:09:40,932 --> 01:09:43,101
Send two copies to Finance.

730
01:11:16,278 --> 01:11:20,031
You can't borrow any more chairs!
There's only one left in here now

731
01:11:20,115 --> 01:11:22,617
-and I need that to sit on!
- What?

732
01:11:25,662 --> 01:11:26,872
Sorry.

733
01:11:28,206 --> 01:11:29,666
Who are you?

734
01:11:30,375 --> 01:11:32,544
My name's Lowry. Sam Lowry.

735
01:11:32,711 --> 01:11:35,505
Oh, yes, the new boy from next door.

736
01:11:37,174 --> 01:11:39,426
My name's Lime. Harvey Lime.

737
01:11:40,135 --> 01:11:41,928
Welcome to Expediting.

738
01:11:42,053 --> 01:11:45,474
Would you mind
if I borrowed your computer console?

739
01:11:45,557 --> 01:11:48,310
I'll let you have it back in 10 minutes.

740
01:11:48,393 --> 01:11:51,480
You want to take my console
into your office?

741
01:11:51,855 --> 01:11:54,107
Yes, just for a few minutes.

742
01:11:54,274 --> 01:11:55,984
I'll tell you what,

743
01:11:56,067 --> 01:11:59,404
you tell me what you want,
and I'll do it for you.

744
01:12:00,572 --> 01:12:02,866
I'm a bit of a whiz on that thing.

745
01:12:05,577 --> 01:12:06,953
All right.

746
01:12:08,872 --> 01:12:12,459
- I need information on a woman named...
- A woman?

747
01:12:13,043 --> 01:12:14,503
- I see.
- Yes.

748
01:12:14,836 --> 01:12:18,840
I know her age and distinguishing marks,
but I need an address, her place of work.

749
01:12:18,924 --> 01:12:21,176
This your dream girl, is it?

750
01:12:24,054 --> 01:12:26,348
Look, let me use your console.

751
01:12:26,765 --> 01:12:28,558
